s', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/88ba7882-2ff9-5786-aa85-47b8ef84dfc1', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88ba7882-2ff9-5786-aa85-47b8ef84dfc1'}, {'abstract': 'Marilyn Stasio’s Crime column stalks a lonely shop clerk in Norway, an almost kidnapped California bank clerk and a jittery Manhattan apartment sitter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/02/books/review/crime-fiction.html', 'snippet': 'Marilyn Stasio’s Crime column stalks a lonely shop clerk in Norway, an almost kidnapped California bank clerk and a jittery Manhattan apartment sitter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Karin Fossum’s characters are so realistic, I keep expecting to see them on the crosstown express. Ragna Reigel, the protagonist of THE WHISPERER (Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, $24), certainly looks familiar. She takes the same bus and sits in the same seat (“by the window three seats back from the driver”) every day on her way to and from work at a supermarket on the outskirts of the small Norwegian city of Kirkelina, where she’s lived since childhood. She buys the same groceries at the same small Turkish shop in her neighborhood, and makes the same simple meals for dinner. “Her world had to be small and manageable, because then she was in control.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Crime/04Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 984, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Crime/04Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 984}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/books/review/04Crime/04Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 625,