one}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d939b7c-4d93-5f55-af6d-81a339a602a6', 'word_count': 1693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d939b7c-4d93-5f55-af6d-81a339a602a6'}, {'abstract': 'The technology would allow fetuses to develop outside the female womb so women would no longer have to be pregnant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/03/opinion/sunday/abortion-technology-debate.html', 'snippet': 'The technology would allow fetuses to develop outside the female womb so women would no longer have to be pregnant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Could an emerging technology reshape the battle lines in the abortion debate? Since Roe v. Wade was decided in 1973, that fight has been defined by the interlocking, absolute values of choice and life: For some, a woman’s right to choose trumps any claim to a right to life by the fetus; for others, it’s the reverse. But what if we could separate those two — what if a woman’s choice to terminate a pregnancy no longer meant terminating the fetus itself?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04istvan/04istvan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04istvan/04istvan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04istvan/04istvan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1023, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04istvan/04istvan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2046, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04istvan/04istvan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04istvan/04istvan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04istvan/04istvan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Abortion Debate Is Stuck. Are Artificial Wombs the Answer?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Artificial Womb Revolution', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-03T17:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zoltan Istvan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zoltan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Istvan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/fddd0d36-5b58-5ed7-8029-398074a87fc6', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fddd0d36-5b58-5ed7-8029-398074a87fc6'}, {'abstract': 'I bought them online. They’re easy to get, and they’ll change everything.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/03/opinion/abortion-pill.html', 'snippet': 'I bought them online. They’re easy to get, and they’ll change everything.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One afternoon about a year ago, just as the Senate began considering Brett Kavanaugh’s nomination to the Supreme Court, I logged on to Day Night Healthcare, an online pharmacy based in India, and ordered a pack of abortion pills. A few hours later, I got a call from a Day Night customer-service agent with a warning. If my credit-card company called to ask about the purchase, “tell them you approve the charge, but don’t say what it’s for,” the man advised. “If they ask, say it’s gym equipment, or something like that.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04manjoo-sub/04manjoo-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04manjoo-sub/04manjoo-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04manjoo-sub/04manjoo-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04manjoo-sub/04manjoo-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04manjoo-sub/04manjoo-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04manjoo-sub/04manjoo-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/04/opinion/sunday/04manjoo-sub/04manjoo-sub-thumbLarge.j