'2019-08-06T15:56:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victor Mather', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mather', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60298100-2e4e-58b2-87b3-a01c1e6c6652', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60298100-2e4e-58b2-87b3-a01c1e6c6652'}, {'abstract': 'A move for Bosie Tea Parlor, the opening of David Chang’s seaport district bar, and more restaurant news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/06/dining/nyc-restaurant-news.html', 'snippet': 'A move for Bosie Tea Parlor, the opening of David Chang’s seaport district bar, and more restaurant news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hawker fare, or street food, is what you’ll find at the new Singaporean restaurant from Salil Mehta and his wife, Stacey Mehta. “Street food, hawker food, represents Singapore,” Mr. Mehta said. “Singapore is a melting pot of cuisines — Chinese, Thai, Indian, Malaysian — but it’s not particularly well represented in New York.” The difference in this new restaurant is that the setting is not in the typical collection of stalls that usually sell street food. It stands alone with elegant velvet upholstery, marble tables and vintage touches. “I wanted to cater to the Gramercy neighborhood and have a beautiful setting, a place where you’d want to enjoy a bottle of wine,” he said. “Most restaurants with food from Singapore and Malaysia don’t have that.” Mr. Mehta is also an owner of Laut, a Malaysian restaurant nearby. Among the dishes at the new restaurant are curry puffs with potato, chicken and beef satays, crispy rice salad with vegetables, sweet and spicy chile crab, and Hainanese chicken with rice, chiles, garlic, shallots and soy sauce. Both the crab and the chicken are signature Singaporean dishes. As for that bottle of wine alongside, it may take a while: The restaurant’s alcohol license is not expected until the end of September. (Opens Friday) ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/dining/06off/merlin_158791674_27274fba-01c6-4eb0-a41e-bf64828e6d0b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/07/dining/06off/merlin_158791674_27274fba-01c6-4eb0-a41e-bf64828e6d0b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/dining/06off/merlin_158791674_27274fba-01c6-4eb0-a41e-bf64828e6d0b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/dining/06off/merlin_158791674_27274fba-01c6-4eb0-a41e-bf64828e6d0b-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1339, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/dining/06off/06off-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/07/dining/06off/06off-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/dining/06off/06off-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Singaporean Street Food in a Beautiful Gramercy Space', 'kicker': 'off the menu', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Freek's Mill (Brooklyn, NY, Restaurant)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bosie Tea Parlor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ole & Steen (Manhattan, NY, Bakery)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Singapura (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-06T15:57:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e65fc635-6026-5385-bdc4-72369e1df21d', 'word_count': 868, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e65fc635-6026-5385-bdc4-72369e1df21d'}, {'abstract': 'He’s no foe of bigotry. He’s an agent of it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/06/opinion/trump-el-paso-racism.html', 'snippet': 'He’s no foe of bigotry. He’s an agent of it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a president orders up a special script, summons the national media and sends a message to all Americans that the “sinister ideologies” of “racism, bigotry and white supremacy” have no place here, the normal response is to cheer.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/06/opinion/06bruni/06bruni-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/06/opinion/06bruni/06bruni-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u