00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sylvie Kauffmann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sylvie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kauffmann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f48d29e4-20d7-52a8-b025-495fb5006f47', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f48d29e4-20d7-52a8-b025-495fb5006f47'}, {'abstract': 'Members of the cast of “Beverly Hills, 90210” reunite on Fox. And “American Hustle” airs on FXM.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/arts/television/whats-on-tv-wednesday-bh90210-and-american-hustle.html', 'snippet': 'Members of the cast of “Beverly Hills, 90210” reunite on Fox. And “American Hustle” airs on FXM.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BH90210 9 p.m. on Fox. “Beverly Hills, 90210,” the genre-defining ’90s prime-time teenage soap opera, ages up and takes a turn for the meta in this quasi-reboot. The new show assembles members of the original cast, including Jennie Garth, Tori Spelling, Jason Priestley and Brian Austin Green. For six episodes, “BH90210” evokes mockumentary (as well as the “Seinfeld” reunion that once happened on “Curb Your Enthusiasm”) with a plot that involves the performers playing versions of themselves who are trying to get the show back off the ground. “We felt like we needed something different because everyone’s doing reboots,” Garth recently told The Los Angeles Times. “This show was groundbreaking when it started, so we were trying to think of what could be the new version of that.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/arts/07tvcol-bhnineotwoone/merlin_158910153_aad32838-7aef-4043-b449-df6a82691131-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop