: 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-07T09:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Express', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mihir Zaveri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mihir', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zaveri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9c1751f-24ec-5ccb-bb9a-683a83459958', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9c1751f-24ec-5ccb-bb9a-683a83459958'}, {'abstract': 'Did Jimi Hendrix perform “The Star-Spangled Banner” for thousands, and did Joni Mitchell sing about her experience at the festival? A few key fact checks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/arts/music/woodstock-myths-debunked.html', 'snippet': 'Did Jimi Hendrix perform “The Star-Spangled Banner” for thousands, and did Joni Mitchell sing about her experience at the festival? A few key fact checks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fifty years after Woodstock, legends about the music festival are tightly braided with the reality of its three chaotic days. The 1969 show was muddy enough (literally and figuratively) for many people to believe both the myths and the debunking, sometimes simultaneously. Even the name of Woodstock doesn’t hold up to cursory fact-checking, as most people know — while the name of Woodstock, N.Y., was more marketable because of the town’s association with Bob Dylan, the event happened more than 40 miles away, in Bethel. Here are five other Woodstock stories that, like the brown acid, turned out to be problematic. ', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/06/arts/flower-promo/flower-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 60