e': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Style', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terri Pous', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pous', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/1b5d70f0-d199-5775-88aa-2422902d8c92', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/1b5d70f0-d199-5775-88aa-2422902d8c92'}, {'abstract': 'Subjugating an entire people wasn’t enough for Prime Minister Narendra Modi.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/opinion/kashmir-india.html', 'snippet': 'Subjugating an entire people wasn’t enough for Prime Minister Narendra Modi.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAHORE, Pakistan — Pakistani kids are taught in and out of school that Kashmir is our “shah rug” (jugular vein). Indians believe that Kashmir is their “atoot ang” (indispensable body part). Urdu and Persian poetry is full of paeans to the beauty of Kashmir. If there is paradise on earth, “it is this, it is this, it is this,” the 14th-century poet Amir Khusro wrote. Since the time of Partition, 72 years ago, India and Pakistan have been fighting wars over Kashmir and calling each other the occupier and the oppressor of the Kashmiris.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/opinion/07hanif-illo/merlin_158992902_c62215f8-4f1f-4f2a-b294-d97fb8018e35-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/07/opinion/07hanif-illo/merlin_158992902_c62215f8-4f1f-4f2a-b294-d97fb8018e35-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 343}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/opinion/07hanif-illo/merlin_158992902_c62215