tions', 'value': 'Lahore (Pakistan)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Delhi (India)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-07T15:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohammed Hanif', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohammed', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hanif', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0259af17-f078-5b8b-a429-b6f8b468538f', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0259af17-f078-5b8b-a429-b6f8b468538f'}, {'abstract': 'Like the mobs that killed Americans of Mexican descent across the West in the early 20th century, the El Paso shooting suspect believed that Texas was better off as a territory run by, and for, white people.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/opinion/el-paso-attack-history.html', 'snippet': 'Like the mobs that killed Americans of Mexican descent across the West in the early 20th century, the El Paso shooting suspect believed that Texas was better off as a territory run by, and for, white people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the cities and towns of California, Colorado, Arizona and other Western states, there are countless highways of memory leading back to El Paso. That Texas border town on the Rio Grande, site of a horrific mass shooting on Saturday, is the Ellis Island of the American Southwest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/opinion/07Tobar/merlin_158925498_8f5db6df-9dc4-44a1-80c2-35da820d8b93-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/07/opinion/07Tobar/merlin_158925498_8f5db6df-9dc4-44a1-80c2-35da820d