'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62fed6c4-35de-5946-82d2-a41248fc512e', 'word_count': 1167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62fed6c4-35de-5946-82d2-a41248fc512e'}, {'abstract': 'Combining psychedelic motifs with precious stones, Brent Neale Winston is redefining the limits of her craft.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/t-magazine/brent-neale-jewelry-designer.html', 'snippet': 'Combining psychedelic motifs with precious stones, Brent Neale Winston is redefining the limits of her craft.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the New York jeweler Brent Neale Winston titled her debut fine jewelry collection After the Rain, it was an emotional response to what she calls “a really bad year.” During months of prescribed bed rest while pregnant with her twin daughters, Winston had channeled her energy into the research and development of a new line of jewelry, resulting in cheery rainbow, cloud and unicorn shapes in pastel semiprecious stones. “I wanted to make pieces that made me smile and were joyful — with as much color as I could possibly use,” she says of the collection, which introduced the Brent Neale jewelry line with an exclusive trunk show for the online retailer Moda Operandi in 2017.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/08/t-magazine/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/08/t-magazine/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/08/t-magazine/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/08/t-magazine/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/08/t-magazine/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/08/t-magazine/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/08/t-magazine/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-copy/05tmag-neale-slide-TIM5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Designer Bringing Whimsy, and Magic Mushrooms, to Fine Jewelry', 'kicker': 'T Presents', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winston, Brent Neale', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jewels and Jewelry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diamonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-07T16:30:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Thawley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thawley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75f14c44-90bb-528e-a28e-cef8c5c40b09', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75f14c44-90bb-528e-a28e-cef8c5c40b09'}, {'abstract': 'Diedrick Brackens’s vibrant textiles tell stories about being black and queer in the South.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/t-magazine/diedrick-brackens-artist.html', 'snippet': 'Diedrick Brackens’s vibrant textiles tell stories about being black and queer in the South.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the first day of weaving class as an undergraduate at the University of North Texas, in Denton, the artist Diedrick Brackens walked into his classroom to find 30 looms lined up in perfect rows in front of a cabinet filled with color-coordinated yarn, like a “rainbow in the back,” he remembers. He was instantly hooked. Now 30, Brackens works from a studio in the Leimert Park neighborhood of Los Angeles, where he makes intricate weavings mainly from cotton, a material he uses for its versatility, he says, but also “to pay tribute to those who came before me.” His grandmother picked cotton as a child in Limestone County, Tex., where she and much of the rest of Brackens’s family later settled. “She had to do this thing that comes along with this awful history,” he says. “And if that is part of my story, I have to make very beautiful things.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/03/t-magazine/art/brackens-slide-9G4L/brackens-slide-9G4L-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 771, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/06/03/t-magazine/art/brackens-slide-9G4L/brackens-slide-9G4L-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 771}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/03/t-magazine/art/brackens-slide-9G4L/brackens-sli