 None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7ebb1c7-5b76-54fc-80eb-feff75129c6d', 'word_count': 817, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7ebb1c7-5b76-54fc-80eb-feff75129c6d'}, {'abstract': 'For their two-year-old fashion brand, Emma Chopova and Laura Lowena transform dead-stock textiles into intricately handcrafted skirts and dresses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/t-magazine/chopova-lowena-fashion.html', 'snippet': 'For their two-year-old fashion brand, Emma Chopova and Laura Lowena transform dead-stock textiles into intricately handcrafted skirts and dresses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a South London industrial park, an overstuffed garment bag spills onto the floor of the light-filled studio of the fashion designers Emma Chopova and Laura Lowena. Its tartan contents are samples from their Chopova Lowena fashion line, just returned from a photo shoot, explains Chopova, as she swoops down and plucks a mini-kilt from the bag.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F/05tmag-chopova-slide-Y41F-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Designers Giving New Life to Forgotten Bulgarian Fabrics', 'kicker': 'T Presents', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chopova Lowena (Fashion Label)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chopova, Emma', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lowena, Laura', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Saint Martins College of Art and Design', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-07T16:30:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Grace Cook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Grace', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cook', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23088d77-f4b6-5cb4-86a7-cb5ae0c9f23c', 'word_count': 871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23088d77-f4b6-5cb4-86a7-cb5ae0c9f23c'}, {'abstract': 'In his studio in Harlem, Sourabh Gupta crafts delicate daisies, roses and lady’s slippers from paper, sponge and even bread.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/t-magazine/papercliff-studios.html', 'snippet': 'In his studio in Harlem, Sourabh Gupta crafts delicate daisies, roses and lady’s slippers from paper, sponge and even bread.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the designer Sourabh Gupta’s studio, in a nondescript apartment building in East Harlem, flowers bloom on nearly every surface. Towers of hollyhock animate one corner with their showy hot-pink-and-white blossoms. On a nearby bookshelf, pale lady’s slippers, Carolina roses and strawberry buds spring from earthenware pots. Gupta moves about gently tending to his nursery — not with pruning shears and trowels, but with tweezers and a magnifying glass. Only up close is it clear that these perennials are all made of paper, stunningly lifelike down to each delicate pistil and stamen.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC/05tmag-gupta-slide-Z6HC-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Designer Who Makes Impossibly Lifelike Flowers From Everyday Materials', 'kicker': 'T Presents', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Designer; A Paper Garden', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gupta, Sourabh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burch, Tory', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-07T16:30:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mimi Vu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mimi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47f2f16a-e2dd-5721-af67-43d90b47e9db', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47f2f16a-e2dd-5721-af67-43d90b47e9db'}, {'abstract': 'Aaron Aujla and Ben Bloomstein of Green River Project draw on their backgrounds as artists to create conceptual handcrafted furniture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/t-magazine/green-river-project-aaron-aujla-ben-bloomstein.html', 'snippet': 'Aaron Aujla and Ben Bloomstein of Green River Project draw on their backgrounds as artists to create conceptual handcrafted furniture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For designers whose work centers on the home, Ben Bloomstein and Aaron Aujla — who make raw, sculptural furniture under the name Green River Project — don’t particularly like staying in one place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB/05tmag-aujla-slide-HIPB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The New York Furniture Studio That’s Become a Fashion World Favorite', 'kicker': 'T presents', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green River Project', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aujla, Aaron', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomstein, Benjamin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-07T16:30:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Merrell Hambleton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Merrell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hambleton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/895fe3c2-73bb-5a01-82a3-f775314060ec', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/895fe3c2-73bb-5a01-82a3-f775314060ec'}, {'abstract': 'Alana Wilson’s delicate vessels belie the violent chemical processes she uses to make them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/t-magazine/alana-wilson-ceramics.html', 'snippet': 'Alana Wilson’s delicate vessels belie the violent chemical processes she uses to make them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alana Wilson, an Australian artist, has always been interested in decay — the ways gravity, heat and time alter and transform. On childhood hikes through the varied landscape of Wellington, New Zealand, where she grew up, Wilson would pocket shells and small animal bones. To the artist, these objects were relics of “the physicality and fragility of the world.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-copy/05tmag-wilson-slide-IKAV-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Sydney, a Ceramic Artist Who Captures the Beauty of Decay', 'kicker': 'T Presents', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Alana (Artist)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ceramics and Pottery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-07T16:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Merrell Hambleton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Merrell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hambleton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32ed48f3-bbed-535b-8895-129bf4a17bb8', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32ed48f3-bbed-535b-8895-129bf4a17bb8'}, {'abstract': 'Tomo Koizumi’s vibrant, ruffled pieces captivated New York last season. Now, he prepares to present his fantastical sophomore collection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/t-magazine/tomo-koizumi-fashion.html', 'snippet': 'Tomo Koizumi’s vibrant, ruffled pieces captivated New York last season. Now, he prepares to present his fantastical sophomore collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A fairy godmother, an overnight transformation, polyester turned into once-in-a-lifetime ball gowns. It may sound cliché, but for Tomo Koizumi, it became reality. For the last seven years, the 31-year-old Japanese designer, who is based in Tokyo, has been quietly making radically vibrant dresses created from acres of organza in topiary-like shapes and colors that run the gamut of the rainbow. Though pop stars including Lady Gaga and the Japanese girl group Perfume have worn his pieces, Koizumi doesn’t sell his clothes in stores and, until recently, he had never presented them during a fashion week. Accordingly, he remained largely unknown outside of Japan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/05/t-magazine/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-copy/05tmag-koizumi-slide-XWGS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Designer Making Otherworldly Dresses From Candy-Colored Organza', 'kicker':