e', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc095ccd-a668-5463-ba11-0cf16ca3b444', 'word_count': 912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc095ccd-a668-5463-ba11-0cf16ca3b444'}, {'abstract': 'The magma mysteriously drained from the crevice last year, and now scorching pools of water are bubbling up from below.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/07/science/hawaii-kilauea-volcano-crater-lake.html', 'snippet': 'The magma mysteriously drained from the crevice last year, and now scorching pools of water are bubbling up from below.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last spring, Hawaii’s Kilauea volcano began its most destructive eruption in recorded history. On May 2, as its underlying magma supply headed to the mountain’s lower east rift zone, a lava lake within the Halema’uma’u summit crater that had been there for 10 years began to rapidly drain. A week later, this pool of molten fury had vanished from sight.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/science/07TB-CRATERLAKE3/07TB-CRATERLAKE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/07/science/07TB-CRATERLAKE3/07TB-CRATERLAKE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 449}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/07/science/07TB-CRATERLAKE3/07TB-CRATERLAKE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im