: 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dead Man Walking (Opera)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dead Man Walking (Movie)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-10T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Penelope Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Penelope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3616840-e2d3-5a3b-90b9-600c60e70733', 'word_count': 2121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3616840-e2d3-5a3b-90b9-600c60e70733'}, {'abstract': 'What began as a method of improving one’s life has become something much kinder — and stranger.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/10/style/self-care/when-did-self-help-become-self-care.html', 'snippet': 'What began as a method of improving one’s life has become something much kinder — and stranger.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Wellness” is a word that has come to encompass our latest dominant sociocultural obsession — how to take care of ourselves in the world. It may, at one point, have been popularly understood as an extension of self-help, a category of literature and speaker circuits that is devoted to personal optimization (and often, productivity). But more recently, under the potent influence of millennial values, wellness has been positioned and marketed as self-care. This wellness is softer, gentler, more forgiving than its self-flagellating forebear. Definitely more fun.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/11/fashion/10SELFHELP-marianne-williamson/merlin_158672925_0b5c1805-94d6-43ae-a701-edea5915a3af-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width'