 1}, {'firstname': 'Karan', 'middlename': 'Deep', 'lastname': 'Singh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Sameer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yasir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37d06c12-4a56-5c08-a007-916efa2cb12d', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37d06c12-4a56-5c08-a007-916efa2cb12d'}, {'abstract': 'Even cooking experts relax their standards once they have kids. We talked to five cooking pros to get tips on how to get dinner on the table while parenting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/14/smarter-living/wirecutter/when-you-love-to-cook-but-also-have-a-kid-embrace-the-microwave.html', 'snippet': 'Even cooking experts relax their standards once they have kids. We talked to five cooking pros to get tips on how to get dinner on the table while parenting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ll never forget when I realized that I was cooking dinner all wrong. My hungry preschooler had just thrown a Category 5 meltdown while I was making burritos — which took me about 30 minutes, using a half-dozen pots and dishes. When my husband came home later, he dumped the cold beans, rice, and cheese on a tortilla, microwaved it, and peacefully sat down to eat — all within two minutes. Why hadn’t I thought of the microwave? This, I realized, was how to do burrito night!', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/20/smarter-living/00wc-cookkids/00wc-cookkids-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/20/smarter-living/00wc-cookkids/00wc-cookkids-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp