word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/ca9e5f63-390d-593d-a5ab-d4720291865e'}, {'abstract': 'Long associated with European cities, the style has plenty of history in other parts of the world, too. In Brazil, it reached a surprising apotheosis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/15/t-magazine/tropical-brutalism.html', 'snippet': 'Long associated with European cities, the style has plenty of history in other parts of the world, too. In Brazil, it reached a surprising apotheosis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last October, the Brazilian architect Paulo Mendes da Rocha, who was nearing 90, was interviewed by the Spanish newspaper El País about his six-decade career. By then, Mendes da Rocha — whose buildings include São Paulo’s subterranean sculpture museum, Museu Brasileiro da Escultura e Ecologia (MuBE), completed in 1995, with its 197-foot stretch of gravity-defying concrete that spans the open plaza, and the levitating concrete disk of the city’s Paulistano Athletic Club Gymnasium, built in 1957 — had been canonized as not only the most important living architect in Brazil, or perhaps in all of Latin America, but also as the world’s most significant practitioner of Brutalism — a word, ironically, that he has always disavowed. “Ask an intellectual what they mean by Brutalism, and the majority won’t know,” Mendes da Rocha told the reporter. “Brutalism is nothing.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18photo/18tmag-brutalism-slide-AXZE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18photo/18tmag-brutalism-slide-AXZE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18photo/18tmag-brutalism-slide-AXZE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18photo/18tmag-brutalism-slide-AXZE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18photo/18tmag-brutalism-slide-AXZE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18photo/18tmag-brutalism-slide-AXZE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18photo/18tmag-brutalism-slide-AXZE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Unexpectedly Tropical History of Brutalism', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buildings (Structures)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Edificio Copan (Sao Paulo, Brazil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Le Corbusier', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Niemeyer, Oscar', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rudolph, Paul', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mendes da Rocha, Paulo', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-15T13:01:2