hrough Burma in search of an extraordinarily large ruby, changed her destiny.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2019/08/16/t-magazine/of-a-kind-marie-helene-de-taillac.html', 'snippet': 'Reading “La Vallée des Rubis,” Joseph Kessel’s 1955 memoir of his odyssey through Burma in search of an extraordinarily large ruby, changed her destiny.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reading “La Vallée des Rubis,” Joseph Kessel’s 1955 memoir of his odyssey through Burma in search of an extraordinarily large ruby, changed her destiny.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1333, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM/18tmag-taillac-slide-3MJM-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Of a Kind: Marie-Hélène de Taillac', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2019-08-16T16:20:51+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/a245c425-3549-5f49-bf06-80d9d3f4e369', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/a245c425-3549-5f49-bf06-80d9d3f4e369'}, {'abstract': 'T’s roundup of people, places and things to know now.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/16/t-magazine/louis-barthelemy-tapestries-nazuna-kyoto-gosho-culture-fashion-news.html', 'snippet': 'T’s roundup of people, places and things to know now.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five years ago, the French textile designer and illustrator Louis Barthélemy was developing prints for Salvatore Ferragamo while splitting his time between Paris and Marrakesh, where he liked to wander the souks in search of inspiration. On a trip to Tangier, he came across “Mère et Fils,” a 2014 book of photographs by the now 61-year-old French artist Denis Dailleux, who lived in Egypt for many years. The images, at once erotic and tender, were of shirtless male Egyptian bodybuilders photographed with their mothers. Barthélemy, now 30, was so struck by them that he decided to go to Cairo himself, where he fell in love with the chaos of the city. He later moved there, into a spacious but faded ’60s-era flat. One day, he visited a tiny stall south of Bab Zuwayla in Suq al-Khayamiya (“Tentmakers’ Market”), where an artisan named Tarek Abdelhay Hafez Abouelenin was hand-making a khayamiya — a vibrant quiltlike work featuring ornate appliqué patterns sewn onto a plain cotton canvas backing, a textile that has been used to cover the insides of tents at weddings, celebrations and funerals for over a millennium. Not surprisingly, the meticulous, time-consuming work is a dying art (most contemporary khayamiya are digitally printed), which made Abouelenin’s work all the more extraordinary. The two men agreed to collaborate on a different sort of khayamiya, one that would stray from traditional arabesque and calligraphic motifs — and would defy the Islamic taboo against both realistic representation and homoeroticism.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-tapestries/18tmag-tapestries-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-tapestries/18tmag-tapestries-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-tapestries/18tmag-tapestries-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-tapestries/18tmag-tapestries-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-tapestries/18tmag-tapestries-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-tapestries/18tmag-tapestries-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-tapestries/18tmag-tapestries-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Erotic Tapestries, a Kyoto Inn Inspired by Sweets and More', 'kicker': 'Notes on the Culture', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Handicrafts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barthelemy, Louis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ferragamo, Salvatore, Italia SpA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beirut (Lebanon)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cairo (Egypt)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Marrakesh (Morocco)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Giza (Egypt)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-16T16:21:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c04fa899-afc6-573b-b4f4-0c48307db98c', 'word_count': 1385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c04fa899-afc6-573b-b4f4-0c48307db98c'}, {'abstract': 'Of the many misconceptions about the fashion industry, the most persistent is that it’s glamorous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/16/t-magazine/ts-fall-womens-fashion-issue-the-tribe.html', 'snippet': 'Of the many misconceptions about the fashion industry, the most persistent is that it’s glamorous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OF THE MANY misconceptions about the fashion industry, the most persistent is that it’s glamorous. There are obvious reasons why this myth has not only endured but, thanks to Instagram, intensified: chief among them the industry’s apparent commitment to gamely fulfilling the public’s fantasy, in part by emphasizing the runway show — the 20 minutes of true spectacle that represent thousands of hours of unseen and unheralded labor — above all else. But anyone who has worked in or knows someone who works in fashion, from the humblest tailor to the starriest creative director, also knows that the business in fact bears a strong resemblance to the restaurant industry, another realm where the risks (and rate of failure) are high, the payoff is usually small, and in which dedication, talent, financial savvy and personality can be nothing against the vicissitudes of luck.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-americancouture-slide-R3FG/18tmag-americancouture-slide-R3FG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 746, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-americancouture-slide-R3FG/18tmag-americancouture-slide-R3FG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 746}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-americancouture-slide-R3FG/18tmag-americancouture-slide-R3FG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 824, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-magazine/18tmag-americancouture-slide-R3FG/18tmag-americancouture-slide-R3FG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1648, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/t-ma