 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cfdf881-1f9b-5ba1-b2ba-714ee60ae53b', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cfdf881-1f9b-5ba1-b2ba-714ee60ae53b'}, {'abstract': 'The blame lies not with Russian hackers but with the presidential candidates and the federal government.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/18/opinion/democrats-donor-data.html', 'snippet': 'The blame lies not with Russian hackers but with the presidential candidates and the federal government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the largest single disclosures of small-donor information in American history took place on July 31. On that day, the names, addresses, occupations and employers of at least 2.3 million people who contributed money to 2020 Democratic presidential candidates were listed on the internet for all to see. The culprits were not Russian hackers or disgruntled tech workers, but the Democratic presidential candidates and the federal government.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/opinion/18primo-privacy/18primo-privacy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 373, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/18/opinion/18primo-privacy/18primo-privacy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 373}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/18/opinion/18primo-privacy/18primo-privacy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 