e': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48079343-ea6a-573b-9195-e110aee52367', 'word_count': 1265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48079343-ea6a-573b-9195-e110aee52367'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of recent poetry books; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/20/books/review/new-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of recent poetry books; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN HER FEMININE SIGN, by Dunya Mikhail. (New Directions, paper, $14.95.) A refugee from Saddam Hussein’s Iraq, Mikhail now lives in Detroit. With plain-spoken clarity, these poems navigate the meaning of home. “How many departures can you put up with?” one asks.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/books/review/25NewNoteworthy/25NewNoteworthy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 293, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/25/books/review/25NewNoteworthy/25NewNoteworthy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 293}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/books/review/25NewNoteworthy/25NewNoteworthy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/books/review/25NewNoteworthy/25NewNoteworthy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 1434, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/books/review/25NewNoteworthy/25NewNoteworthy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/25/books/review/25NewNoteworthy/25NewNoteworthy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/books/review/25NewNoteworthy/25NewNoteworthy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New & Noteworthy Poetry From James Tate, Jana Prikryl and More', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New &amp; Noteworthy; Poetry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-20T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0048248-3827-579b-94ed-de18f17d16ed', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0048248-3827-579b-94ed-de18f17d16ed'}, {'abstract': 'Include handles. Use cushioned seats. Make sure the upside person won’t be too earthbound.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/20/magazine/how-to-build-a-teeter-totter.html', 'snippet': 'Include handles. Use cushioned seats. Make sure the upside person won’t be too earthbound.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Actions that take place on one side of a teeter totter have direct consequence on the other side,” says Ronald Rael, a professor of architecture at the University of California, Berkeley. For about 40 minutes on July 28, people on both sides of the United States-Mexico border, where New Mexico meets Ciudad Juárez, rode three bright pink teeter totters affixed to the towering steel border wall that were designed and installed by Rael and the architect and professor Virginia San Fratello. In planning your teeter totter, make sure that when one side is touching the ground, the other side will be twice as tall as the fulcrum in the middle. Don’t send the upside person dangerously high in the air, but don’t keep him or her overly earthbound either. “Part of the thrill is that your feet aren’t touching the ground,” San Fratello says.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/magazine/25Mag-Tip-1/25Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 180, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/25/magazine/25Mag-Tip-1/25Mag-Tip-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 180}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/magazine/25Mag-Tip-1/25Mag-Tip-1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 307, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/magazine/25Mag-Tip-1/25Mag-Tip-1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/magazine/25Mag-Tip-1/25Mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/25/magazine/25Mag-Tip-1/25Mag-Tip-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit'