d_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24a23ce4-cbbe-5efa-af2e-5baf0a03d116'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on the distinction between “equal” and “equitable” when it comes to divvying up the bill.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/20/magazine/i-go-on-family-vacations-with-my-rich-siblings-must-i-pay-the-same.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on the distinction between “equal” and “equitable” when it comes to divvying up the bill.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am considerably younger than my older siblings, and we are close. My sister and brother have had stable, well-paid jobs for the last eight to 12 years. I, on the other hand, have struggled through unpaid or low-wage jobs, though I have been at a better job for over a year. My sister and brother have been generous with me in the past, paying for holidays and clothes, but I am now expected to pay my share when it comes to family holidays and my mother, who is financially dependent on us. I can’t truly afford the same holidays and items that my brother and sister can afford. I also cannot help my mother at the same level they can. What can I do in this circumstance? Should we all shoulder every burden — be it a vacation or my mother’s expenses — equally, or can I ask that we divide the burden proportionally? I also struggle with the idea that I must spend more than I am comfortable spending on holidays with family. How do I broach this subject and illustrate that I should have other priorities — like debt reduction and savings — before spending on luxury or helping others in the family? In the past, broaching the subject of my inability to spend as much as they has elicited frustration and an utter lack of empathy as my spending habits are torn apart. Name Withheld', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-articleLarge-v36.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-articleLarge-v36.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-jumbo-v36.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-superJumbo-v36.jpg', 'height': 1148, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-thumbStandard-v38.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-thumbStandard-v38.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/10/07/magazine/25mag-ethicist-image1/25mag-ethicist-image1-thumbLarge-v38.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I Go on Family Vacations With My Rich Siblings. Must I Pay the Same?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I Go on Family Vacations With My Richer Siblings. Must I Pay the Same?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Relationships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-20T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54f365c3-e0b8-5e42-87fd-ebc4fb7a5b89', 'word_count': 1256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54f365c3-e0b8-5e42-87fd-ebc4fb7a5b89'}, {'abstract': 'He says low-quality streaming is hurting our songs and our brains. Is he right?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/20/magazine/neil-young-streaming-music.html', 'snippet': 'He says low-quality streaming is hurting our songs and our brains. Is he right?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Neil Young is crankier than a hermit being stung by bees. He hates Spotify. He hates Facebook. He hates Apple. He hates Steve Jobs. He hates what digital technology is doing to music. “I’m only one person standing there going, ‘Hey, this is [expletive] up!’\u2009” he shouted, ranting away on the porch of his longtime manager Elliot Roberts’s house overlooking Malibu Canyon in the sunblasted desert north of Los Angeles. The dial thermometer at the far end of the porch indicated that it was now upward of 110 degrees of some kind of heat. Maybe the dial was stuck.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/magazine/25mag-young-pics-1/25mag-young-pics-1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/25/magazine/25mag-young-pics-1/25mag-young-pics-1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight':