lt, Haddon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-20T19:01:24+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Proudfoot', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Proudfoot', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/dc2b2af7-5f6a-5732-af2a-8b7117aae414', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/dc2b2af7-5f6a-5732-af2a-8b7117aae414'}, {'abstract': 'In the late 1960s, Haddon Salt built a fast-food empire. Then Kentucky Fried Chicken came knocking.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/20/opinion/haddon-salt.html', 'snippet': 'In the late 1960s, Haddon Salt built a fast-food empire. Then Kentucky Fried Chicken came knocking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a spring day nearly seven years ago, I stepped away from my desk as C.E.O. of my then-fledgling filmmaking company to grab some lunch with my team in the Los Feliz neighborhood of Los Angeles. Bored with our usual haunts, we landed on H. Salt Esq. Authentic English Fish and Chips, a curious ’70s-style fast-food counter in a sleepy strip mall on Hollywood Boulevard. Stepping inside, I was overwhelmed with the same sensation I had on my first visit to the original Disneyland Park in Anaheim: Someone with a vision built this place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Almost Famous: The King of Fish and Chips', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salt, Haddon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'H Salt of Southern California Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seafood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fast Food Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kentucky Fried Chicken', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanders, Harland', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-20T19:06:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Proudfoot', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Proudfoot', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac2abf23-f41b-5076-a7b6-ee13b77c772f', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac2abf23-f41b-5076-a7b6-ee13b77c772f'}, {'abstract': 'The weariest warblers are more likely to sleep with their heads tucked in — saving energy, but making them more vulnerable to predators.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/20/science/migratory-birds-sleep.html', 'snippet': 'The weariest warblers are more likely to sleep with their heads tucked in — saving energy, but making them more vulnerable to predators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every spring, throngs of garden warblers make a treacherous, multiweek journey from their winter homes in Africa to their summer breeding grounds in Europe. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/19/science/19TB-BIRDNAPS1/19TB-BIRDNAPS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/19/science/19TB-BIRDNAPS1/19TB-BIRDNAPS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/19/science/19TB-BIRDNAPS1/19TB-BIRDNAPS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cr