lso tries to squeeze into a spot on the sand, even if many avoid sunbathing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/22/world/asia/china-beidaihe-beach-communist-party-mao.html', 'snippet': 'China’s top officials still come to Mao’s favorite beach to make fateful decisions, but now, the general public also tries to squeeze into a spot on the sand, even if many avoid sunbathing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIDAIHE, China — Most summers, the founder of Communist China, Mao Zedong, made big and fateful decisions at a funky stretch of beach a few hundred miles east of the nation’s sweltering capital. He swam in weather fair or foul. He sat cross-legged in the sand, dressed only in black trunks, his portly belly exposed for all to see.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/15/world/00china-beach-dispatch-1/merlin_158901627_affd4558-572f-4b8a-8ad3-1bfc413b6e46-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/15/world/00china-beach-dispatch-1/merlin_158901627_affd4558-572f-4b8a-8ad3-1bfc413b6e46-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/15/world/00china-beach-dispatch-1/merlin_158901627_affd4558-572f-4b8a-8ad3-1bfc413b6e46-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/15/world/00china-beach-dispatch-1/merlin_158901627_affd4558-572f-4b8a-8ad3-1bfc413b6e46-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/15/world/00china-beach-dispatch-1/00china-beach-dispatch-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/08/15/world/00china-beach-dispatch-1/00china-beach-dispatch-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/15/world/00china-beach-dispatch-1/00china-beach-dispatch-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Mao’s Beach, China’s Leaders Still Make History as Lifeguards Hide From the Sun', 'kicker': 'China Dispatch', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seeking Surf (but No Sun) With a Side of History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beidaihe (China)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beaches', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mao Zedong', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-22T09:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Perlez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5df23003-b1a7-59b9-85fa-28c2808ac951', 'word_count': 1092, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5df23003-b1a7-59b9-85fa-28c2808ac951'}, {'abstract': 'How to spend a weekend in two of northwest France’s most enchanting cities, with a side-trip to Mont-St.-Michel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/22/travel/what-to-do-36-hours-in-rennes-and-st-malo-france.html', 'snippet': 'How to spend a weekend in two of northwest France’s most enchanting cities, with a side-trip to Mont-St.-Michel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Metropolitan. Maritime. Mystical. Those are the three faces of France’s rugged northwest corner, where Arthurian legends, stone circles and Celtic traditions suffuse the forests and fishing villages. Now they are closer than ever, thanks to the two-year-old LGV train line, which links Paris to the regional capital of Rennes in just 90 minutes. On arrival, you step into an attractive university city of riverside promenades, grand stone squares and historical townhouses with one of France’s largest produce markets (which feeds a copious restaurant scene), along with a dense concentration of bars. A bit north, the walled town of St.-Malo enchants with its storybook streets and ramparts (destroyed in World War II and rebuilt), sea-swept rocky shoreline and proximity to France’s most mystical spot of all: the island of Mont-St.-Michel.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/travel/22Hours-Rennes7/22Hours-Rennes7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/08/25/travel/22Hours-Rennes7/22Hours-Rennes7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/travel/22Hours-Rennes7/22Hours-Rennes7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/25/travel/22Hours-Rennes7/22Hours-Rennes7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJum