', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-08-22T23:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anita Isaacs and Álvaro Montenegro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaacs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Álvaro', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Montenegro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/528a98ad-9ed7-5e08-8e52-4990203c2401', 'word_count': 905, 'uri': 'nyt://article/528a98ad-9ed7-5e08-8e52-4990203c2401'}, {'abstract': 'At a forum for presidential candidates, “everyone counts” sounded like both a moral and an electoral imperative.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/08/22/opinion/native-american-democrats.html', 'snippet': 'At a forum for presidential candidates, “everyone counts” sounded like both a moral and an electoral imperative.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What do the 573 federally recognized nations of American Indians and Alaska Natives all have in common? A never-ending need for lawyers. The Frank LaMere Native American Presidential Forum held this week in Sioux City, Iowa, at which 11 presidential candidates fielded questions from indigenous elected officials and activists, was a rousing two-day argument for an informed, experienced, compassionate and rational president. Sponsored by Four Directions, the South Dakota-based advocates for native voting rights, it was also a sobering reminder that the road to equality in the United States is paved with outrage, elbow grease and paperwork.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/08/22/opinion/22Vowell/merlin_159489375_12657f10-a92d-4273-a6d3-d82315bfd7